          "mechanism": "Ortu et al. 2024 ('Competition of Mechanisms', ACL — gpt2-small primary) show the in-context answer wins via attention from the final-token query (attribute-seeking features) to the counterfactual attribute token in context, localized to a few late heads; modifying two heads' attention flips the factual/counterfactual preference. That is a query-content × key-content conjunction: (relation-completion query features) × (in-context-answer entity features). FRA edits the conjunction in either direction — suppress context override (restore the fact) or enforce it.",
          "why_linear_steer_fails": "Suppressing the counterfactual token's content direction ('Rome') corrupts that entity everywhere — legitimate Rome mentions, Rome as the correct object of other relations in the same context — and a position-gated patch fails because the conflicting statement's position varies per prompt. Only the pair edit distinguishes 'Rome attended-to as the answer of THIS relation' from 'Rome elsewhere'. Caveat to respect: the carrying heads overlap the name-mover family, so the IOI backup risk is live — this is the sharpest available test of whether LBNR failure is about name-moving per se or about the IOI task.",
